  summary: "What would you do if you discovered your shop's scales had been quietly short-changing your customers for months? One grain merchant's answer cost him money — and earned him something worth far more.",
  moral: "Honest trade brings barakah — a blessing in your earnings that no cheating can ever buy.",
  ref: "Based on the Qur'anic command to give full measure and weight and never cheat in the scales (meanings in our own words); story is original fiction written to illustrate the lesson.",
  body: [
    "Honest trade is one of the most beloved acts in Islam. The Qur'an commands traders to give full measure and weight, and warns against cheating in the scales. But what does that look like in real life? This is the story of Kareem, the grain merchant, and the day his old scales told him the truth.",
    "Kareem had sold rice, wheat, and lentils in the old market for twenty years. His shop was small but busy, and people trusted him. His brass scales were older than his shop — a gift from his late father, polished bright by decades of use.",
    "One evening, a trader in the next street showed Kareem his modern digital scale. Out of curiosity, Kareem weighed a bag of rice on both scales. His heart stopped. The old brass scales showed a full kilo — the digital scale showed nine hundred and sixty grams. For months, perhaps years, every customer had received a little less than they paid for.",
    "Kareem sat in his empty shop long after closing, staring at the scales. Nobody knew. Nobody would ever know. He could quietly buy a new scale tomorrow and say nothing — the extra profit had long been spent on his family. But then he remembered his father's words: \"My son, a scale that cheats is a scale that steals. And a thief's money carries no blessing.\"",
    "The next morning, Kareem did something the whole market talked about for years. He placed a sign outside his shop: \"My old scales were wrong. If you bought grain from me, please come — I will return what I owe you.\" Then he opened his account book, found his regular customers, and began visiting them one by one, returning the difference with an apology.",
    "Some laughed and told him to keep it. An old widow cried and said, \"May Allah bless you, my son — in forty years of shopping, no trader has ever come to my door.\" A few angry young men demanded extra, and Kareem paid them too, smiling. By the end of the month, he had returned more money than he had saved all year.",
    "But something strange happened. Word spread through the market like the smell of fresh bread. \"Buy from Kareem,\" people said, \"his scales are the most honest in the city.\" Customers came from streets that had never heard of his shop. Within a year, his small shop had doubled — not because he was clever, but because he was honest.",
    "Lesson: Honesty in trade is not a loss; it is an investment with Allah. Cheating may add coins to your pocket, but it removes barakah from your life. Give full measure, keep your word, and trust that honest earnings carry a blessing no fraud can match.",
    "Daily-life application: (1) In any work — shop, office, or freelance — deliver exactly what you promised, or more. (2) If you discover a mistake that harmed someone, fix it even when no one would know. (3) Never hide defects in what you sell or exaggerate in what you advertise. (4) Remember: the customer you treat honestly today becomes the trust that feeds you tomorrow.",
    "Discussion question: Have you ever been tempted to stay silent about a mistake that benefited you? What did you choose — and what would Kareem have done?",
    "Source note: Based on the Qur'anic command to give full measure and weight and never cheat in the scales (meanings in our own words); story is original fiction written to illustrate the lesson.",
  ],
